Disclosure: I work on Tidy Cents, the tool mentioned at the end.
A customer disputes a $240 order as "not received". The bank pulls the money back, the processor adds a dispute fee, and the goods are already gone. Whether you get the money back depends mostly on one thing: a complete, readable evidence pack sent before the response deadline your processor gives you.
Sellers rarely lose because they had no proof. They lose because the proof was in four places and the deadline passed.
const disputes = [
  { id: "D-1042", amountCents: 24000, openedOn: "2026-11-02", respondByDays: 10,
    evidence: { order: true, tracking: true, delivery: false, messages: true } },
  { id: "D-1047", amountCents: 8900, openedOn: "2026-11-09", respondByDays: 10,
    evidence: { order: true, tracking: false, delivery: false, messages: false } },
];

const DAY = 86_400_000;
const today = new Date("2026-11-10");
const rows = disputes.map((d) => {
  const due = new Date(Date.parse(d.openedOn) + d.respondByDays * DAY);
  const missing = Object.entries(d.evidence).filter(([, ok]) => !ok).map(([k]) => k);
  return { id: d.id, daysLeft: Math.ceil((due - today) / DAY), missing };
}).sort((a, b) => a.daysLeft - b.daysLeft);

Two rules make this list useful:
Sort by days left, not by amount. A small dispute you can still win beats a large one already past its date.
Show what is missing, by name. "delivery" tells you to fetch the carrier's proof of delivery today.
The response window is set by your processor and card network, so store it per dispute instead of hard-coding one number.
We built this into Chargeback Evidence Desk ($19, free demo): every order record, delivery proof and customer message in one file, deadlines tracked, missing items flagged. https://gettidycents.com/product/chargeback-evidence-desk
